{It was now White’s turn to move, and she touched the f-pawn, of course intending f3, to drive off the black bishop, overlooking that the pawn is fixed by Black’s bishop at b6. The penalty was "move your king,"} 1. Kh1 {and then Black played} f3 {, which, it requires very little analysis to prove, wins the game in a few moves against any play:} 2. gxf3 Bxf3+ 3. Kg1 Qg5+ 4. Ng3 Bxd1 {and wins. It was a curious instance, full of point, where the necessary enforcement of a penalty meant nothing less than a lost game for White. It should be added that the more experienced players are not much troubled about the points now referred to, and of course this fact gives them additional advantages. Source: The Times, 26 June 1897} 0-1

