1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Be7 5. Bxc6 dxc6 6. Nxe5 O-O 7. O-O Bd6 8. f4 Ne8 9. Ng4 Qh4 10. h3 Bc5+ 11. Kh1 h5 12. Nh2 Nf6 13. Nf3 Qg3 14. Qe1 Qxe1 {"Miss Field had here a very strong attack if she had properly backed it up. She did wrong in exchanging off her queen."} 15. Rxe1 g6 16. d4 Bb6 17. c3 Kg7 18. Ng5 a6 19. Nd2 Ba7 20. Ndf3 c5 21. Be3 cxd4 22. Bxd4 Bxd4 23. Nxd4 c5 24. Ndf3 b5 25. a3 Bb7 26. Rad1 Rfe8 27. e5 Nd5 28. Kg1 Rad8 29. g3 Nb6 30. Rxd8 Rxd8 31. e6 f6 32. e7 Re8 33. Ne6+ Kf7 34. Nd8+ {Sources: London Evening Standard, 29 June 1897; BCM, August 1897, p295. After the event, the Rothschild best game prize (worth 20GBP) was judged by Pillsbury, and this game was among the three that shared it. (n.b. Freda Sterling Berry - not absolutely certain of her identity but I think she was Sarah Fridzweeda Seymour (1851-1928) but known as Freda, and married Thomas Sterling Berry, who became a bishop, see Wiki entry - all chess references are to Mrs F Sterling Berry)} 1-0

